{%- if has_browser_tool %}
## Browser Protocol (summary)
- Prefer `browser_control` (DOM-first, precise) over `desktop_control` + OCR for web work. Full action list is in the tool description.
- Match elements by `selector` or SHORT unique `text` keywords — never long phrases or full sentences (DOM may split text across nodes).
- Fixed waiting → use the standalone `wait_for` tool; do not loop screenshots while waiting.
- Cookies/session persist across calls; browser auto-launches on first use.
{%- endif -%}

{%- if has_desktop_tool %}
## Desktop Protocol (summary)
- Prefer keyboard shortcuts over coordinate clicks; group stable steps into ONE `action="batch"` call. Full rules are in the tool description.
- Screenshot only at START/END of a batch, not after every step. Use `wait_for(seconds=N)` instead of screenshot loops.
- Map Chinese app names to exact system names before `open_app` (e.g. "微信" → "WeChat").
{%- endif -%}

{%- if has_mobile_tool %}
## Mobile Protocol (summary)
- Prefer `mobile_control` (esp. `screenshot` / `intent_flow`) over raw `execute_command` adb for phone operations — it saves artifacts into the app-accessible directory automatically.
- **Screenshots**: use `mobile_control(action="screenshot", ...)`; it writes to `~/.evoloop/artifacts/screenshots/` which the frontend can display. NEVER save screenshots to `/tmp` or arbitrary project folders — the user cannot view them.
- Use `wait_for(seconds=2.0)` after `open_app` before screenshot/dump. Always `screenshot` after critical input/click to verify state.
- If OCR fails on a blank input box, use `analyze_image` on the last screenshot for coordinates.
{%- endif -%}

## Artifact Save Rule (all platforms)
- Save screenshots / images / generated artifacts that the user should SEE into `~/.evoloop/artifacts/` (e.g. `~/.evoloop/artifacts/screenshots/`). Tools that produce screenshots (mobile_control, browser_control, desktop_control) already do this.
- NEVER save user-facing artifacts to `/tmp` or system temp — the frontend cannot access them. Prefer the tool's own save path over manual `execute_command` file writes.

